GNU inline complete-declaration-chain regression, 2026-09-15.
Source SHA-256: b8e2bd2159dcfc1eb6bfaaac0be9bf3fc2774ea9c1119d652849da4c6a430161
Environment: evo-flight-lab:sealed, linux/arm64, network none.
Fixed compilers: GCC 14.2.0-19 and Clang 19.1.7.

Each oracle:
  $cc -std=gnu11 -O$opt gnu-inline-definition-chain.c -o program
  ./program
GCC O0/O2 and Clang O0/O2: compile 0, program exit 0 in all four cases.

The exploratory print probe used an extern-inline body returning x+100,
a direct caller before the real definition, a real definition returning x+7,
a caller after it, and an indirect call through f's address. With x=2 every
oracle printed "9 9 9" at O0 and O2. The prior subject instead expanded
x+100 into both direct callers: its focused regression exited 1.

After the repair the shared definition-role resolver chooses the real body's
parameter and local-object identities for direct substitution as well as the
out-of-line definition. The checked fixture also verifies that side effects
of the superseded inline-only body do not execute.

Subject debug SHA-256: 2671ad2d8ff56f70228ef3e493576c3a6d6465fe4c3da0b0dd6cca1a11b5cc36
  evocc --target=aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu --emit=asm source.c > source.s
  $cc source.s -o program && ./program
Both GCC and Clang assembly/link/program exits: 0.
Both C-HIR and EvoIR interpreters: 0.
This is a targeted regression, not a full-configuration result.

Related option boundary, same sealed compilers and target:
  $cc -std=gnu89 -fno-gnu89-inline -fsyntax-only -x c /dev/null
GCC exit 1: cc1: error: '-fno-gnu89-inline' is only supported in GNU99 or C99 mode
Clang exit 0.
The profile refuses that final effective combination. A later -std=gnu11
is accepted: options are reduced in their written order before this check.
